<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gil González Dávila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1480–<a href="/years/1526">1526</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gil González Dávila was a Spanish conquistador who led the first Spanish expedition into what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in 1522–1523. He met the chiefs of Nicoya and Nicarao, who accepted baptism, and gathered much gold. Later conflicts with rival Spanish captains over control of Nicaragua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> ended with his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gil-gonzalez-davila.jpg" alt="Gil González Dávila" width="252" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, subject died in 1526 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gil_Gonzalez_Davila.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gil González Dávila first appears in the records in 1508, with a royal commission to examine the accounts and tax records of estates, and in 1511 he was made contador, accountant, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. He came to hold an estate with over 200 Indian slaves, and in 1518 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> a report highly critical of the colony's management. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> the pilot Andrés Niño approached him with a plan to explore the Pacific coast, and with the help of Bishop Juan Rodriguez de Fonseca, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, they won the king's consent in June 1519.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> the governor, Pedrarias, blocked his inspection of the colony's taxes and hindered his search for ships, so González and Niño built four brigantines in the Pearl Islands. They sailed on 26 January 1522, but leaking ships forced González to march west by land while Niño followed the coast. According to the treasurer Andrés de Cereceda, the people of Nicoya offered no resistance and more than 6000 were baptised. González reached the lakes later named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> before attacks forced him back to his ships, and in June 1523 he returned to Panama with three leaking ships, 100 exhausted men and much gold.</p><p>Escaping Pedrarias's attempt to arrest him, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and fitted out a new expedition. In 1524 a storm drove him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, where he named Puerto Caballos and founded San Gil de Buenavista. Marching south, he found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-de-cordoba-founder-of-nicaragua">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba</a>, sent by Pedrarias, held his territory; he captured a force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> by a trick but released it. Cristóbal de Olid, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, then took him prisoner at Naco, until Olid's own men turned on Olid, who was tried and beheaded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>González and Francisco de las Casas declared their loyalty to Cortés and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, where Salazar de la Pedrada, in charge for Cortés, had them arrested and prosecuted for Olid's death. He meant to execute them but was forced to send them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as prisoners. What happened to González there is unknown; his patron Fonseca had died in 1524. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> on 21 April 1526, leaving a son of the same name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gil González Dávila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Spanish expedition of <b>Gil González Dávila</b> reached Nicoya in 1522-23, and the chief accepted baptism.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-nicoya">Chiefdom of Nicoya</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1523 the cacique, known to the Spanish as Nicarao, received <b>Gil González Dávila</b> and asked him searching questions about Christianity, the flood and the end of the world before agreeing to baptism.</blockquote><a href="/state/nicarao-chiefdom">Nicarao chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.65,-4.7&amp;year=1509" aria-label="Ávila on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gil González Dávila"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4696 4088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4696 4088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.65,-4.7&amp;year=1509">What was here in 1509? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
